<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pomerania-Wolgast</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Duchy of Pomerania-Wolgast</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pomerania-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolgast">Wolgast</a> was a partition of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>, created in 1295 for Bogislaw IV and ruled by Griffin dukes from Wolgast. It covered the coastal lands north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peene">Peene</a>, with Hanseatic towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greifswald">Greifswald</a>, and was itself divided several times. It was finally united with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania-stettin">Pomerania-Stettin</a> in 1625, when Philip Julius died and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogislaw-xiv">Bogislaw XIV</a> inherited it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pomerania-wolgast.jpg" alt="Pomerania-Wolgast" width="320" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gustav Droysen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cammin_prince-bishopric_14th_century_(134037786).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
